      "body": "## Overview\n\nMake two CPU kernel tests behave correctly on x86 hosts that only have AVX2 (no AVX512-BF16): one crashed the whole pytest process, the other failed instead of skipping. Test-only change, no runtime code touched.\n\n## Claims\n\n- `tests/kernels/test_cpu_fused_sampling.py` no longer aborts with `Illegal instruction` at collection time on AVX2-only CPUs.\n- `tests/kernels/test_awq_int4_to_int8.py` skips the tests that need the AVX512-BF16-only INT4 ops instead of failing, while still running the tests that do not need them.\n- No behavior change on hosts that have the ops (e.g. the AVX512 CI runners).\n\n## Validation\n\nHost: Intel i5-12400 (AVX2, no AVX512), WSL2 Ubuntu 24.04, CPU nightly wheel `0.32.1rc1.dev36+ge68efc685`, tests taken from this branch (based on `5caabdb`).\n\n```sh\npytest tests/kernels/test_cpu_fused_sampling.py tests/kernels/test_awq_int4_to_int8.py -rs\n```\n\n| Test file | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| `test_cpu_fused_sampling.py` | process killed: `Fatal Python error: Illegal instruction` (exit 132) | 35 passed |\n| `test_awq_int4_to_int8.py` | 13 failed, 3 passed (`AttributeError: '_OpNamespace' '_C' object has no attribute 'convert_weight_packed_scale_zp'`) | 3 passed, 13 skipped |\n\n`pre-commit run --files <both files>` passes.\n\n(I exported `TRITON_DEFAULT_BACKEND=cpu` only because my WSL machine also exposes an NVIDIA driver, see #62484; it is unrelated to this change.)\n\n## Details\n\n**Root cause.** On x86, `CpuPlatform.import_kernels()` picks one of three builds based on the CPU: `_C` (AVX512 + BF16), `_C_AVX512`, or `_C_AVX2`. `test_cpu_fused_sampling.py` hard-coded `import vllm._C`, which loads the AVX512-BF16 build directly and executes unsupported instructions on an AVX2 CPU. `test_awq_int4_to_int8.py` calls `convert_weight_packed_scale_zp` / `int4_scaled_mm_cpu`, which are only registered under `#if defined(__AVX512BF16__) && defined(__AVX512F__)` in `csrc/cpu/torch_bindings.cpp`, but had no guard.\n\n**Fix.**\n- Sampling test: call `current_platform.import_kernels()` instead of importing `vllm._C` directly.\n- AWQ test: add a `requires_int4_cpu_ops` `skipif` marker (same `hasattr(torch.ops._C, ...)` approach as `test_cpu_quant_fused_moe.py`) on the two classes that use the ops. I used a marker rather than a module-level skip so `TestCreateWeightsUnchanged`, which does not need the ops, keeps running.\n\n`vllm._custom_ops` runs `current_platform.import_kernels()` on import and is imported at the top of the AWQ test, so the marker is evaluated after the kernels are loaded.\n\n**Limitations.** I do not have AVX512 hardware, so I could not run the AWQ tests to confirm they still execute where the ops exist; that relies on the reasoning above and on the CPU CI job (`.buildkite/hardware_tests/cpu.yaml`). Other CPU tests in that job also misbehave on my host (e.g. 16 numeric-tolerance failures in `test_cpu_fused_moe.py`); I left them out because I am not sure they are not specific to my setup.\n\n**AI assistance:** This PR was investigated and prepared with an AI assistant (Claude), which also ran the tests above on my machine. The diff is 11 added lines and 1 replaced line across two test files; I have reviewed it and can explain it.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n",
